Answer: theia is an automated analysis technique for deep‑learning programs that examines both the properties of the training data and the structure of the model’s source code. by building a library‑agnostic meta‑model of a program and applying context‑sensitive analyses such as call‑strings and parameter‑sensitive analysis together with a set of verification rules, theia can locate structural defects—issues with activation functions, layer configurations, loss functions, preprocessing, missing or redundant layers, and similar problems—early in the training process. it currently supports models written with keras and pytorch and provides developers with pinpointed bug locations and suggested fixes.

neuralint is a prior tool for detecting bugs in deep‑learning code. it also aims to identify problems in dl programs, but it focuses on a different set of checks and, according to comparative evaluations, discovers fewer structural bugs than theia.
